---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import ButtonLink from '../components/ButtonLink.astro'
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro'
import { INTEGRATIONS, TDARR } from '../data/integrations'

/*
 * The most carefully worded page on the site.
 *
 * Playarr Server reads a library the operator has already organised, using tools the
 * operator already runs. It performs no indexing and obtains no media. Every
 * sentence here must stay on that side of the line — see
 * docs/artifacts/playarr-legal-release.html.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Integrations"
  description="Playarr Server reads the library your existing management tools have already organised — Sonarr, Radarr, Lidarr, Readarr, Bazarr and Prowlarr — and dispatches background encoding to Tdarr."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Integrations"
    title="Works with the tools you already run"
    lede="If you keep your library tidy with the *arr applications, Playarr Server reads the result. It does not replace them, it does not duplicate them, and their own interfaces carry on working exactly as before."
  >
    <div class="statement">
      <p>
        <strong>What Playarr Server takes from them:</strong> the titles, the file paths those titles
        resolve to, and the metadata identifiers already established. That is what turns a folder
        of files into a catalogue with correct posters, correct episode ordering and correct cast
        lists.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What Playarr Server does not do:</strong> it performs no indexing, runs no searches
        against anything outside your own library, and obtains no media. It is a playback,
        catalogue and metadata layer over storage you already control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tint title="Library managers">
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Application</th>
            <th scope="col">Covers</th>
            <th scope="col">What Playarr Server reads</th>
            <th scope="col">If it is not connected</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            INTEGRATIONS.map((integration) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{integration.name}</th>
                <td>{integration.role}</td>
                <td>{integration.reads}</td>
                <td class="muted">{integration.without}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="after">
      Playarr Server builds its catalogue from what these applications have already reconciled, rather
      than scanning folders itself — connect at least one of the four library applications and
      your titles appear.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Transcoding at library scale">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <h3>{TDARR.name}</h3>
        <p>{TDARR.reads}</p>
        <p>
          Keeping the two apart matters. Someone pressing play should never wait behind a queue of
          library maintenance, and library maintenance should never be starved by playback. They
          are separate systems with separate priorities, and background work steps back while
          people are watching.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="stat-card tone-amber" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="80">
        <div class="stat-icon"><Icon name="cpu" /></div>
        <p class="stat-title">Two independent systems</p>
        <ul>
          <li>On-demand — latency-sensitive, triggered by playback</li>
          <li>Background — library-wide, dispatched to Tdarr</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="stat-note">Never sharing a code path. Background work yields while you watch.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tint title="Metadata and artwork">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose" data-reveal>
        <p>
          Artwork and metadata identifiers arrive with the records Playarr Server reads from your
          library managers. Artwork is fetched once and cached on your own disk, so browsing does
          not hammer an upstream service every time a poster scrolls past.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site and any Playarr Server installation showing that imagery carry the required notice
          — see <a href="/legal/licences">licences and attribution</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="stat-card tone-teal" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="80">
        <div class="stat-icon"><Icon name="grid" /></div>
        <p class="stat-title">Fetched once, cached locally</p>
        <ul>
          <li>12 MB ceiling per image</li>
          <li>Served from your own disk after the first fetch</li>
          <li>Source API keys never reach a client</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Setting them up">
    <p class="lede">
      Each connection is a URL and an API key, added from the admin console. The setup guide walks
      through each one and explains what to expect on the first synchronisation.
    </p>
    <p><ButtonLink href="/docs/library-managers">Connect your library managers</ButtonLink></p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .statement {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
    padding: var(--space-6);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .statement p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    text-transform: none;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .muted {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(240px, 320px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
  }

  .prose h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .stat-card {
    padding: var(--space-6);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface-strong);
  }

  .stat-icon {
    display: inline-grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--tone-tint, var(--brand-tint));
    color: var(--tone-color, var(--brand));
  }

  .stat-icon :global(svg) {
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
  }

  .tone-amber {
    --tone-color: var(--hue-amber);
    --tone-tint: var(--hue-amber-tint);
  }
  .tone-teal {
    --tone-color: var(--hue-teal);
    --tone-tint: var(--hue-teal-tint);
  }

  .stat-title {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .stat-card ul {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    padding-inline-start: 1.1em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .stat-card li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .stat-note {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
  }

  .after {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
